diff --git a/docs/data-transfer.md b/docs/data-transfer.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..90b7764
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/data-transfer.md
@@ -0,0 +1,66 @@
+# Exporting and importing Fizzy data
+
+Admins can request a whole-account export in **Account Settings**. Anyone can
+request a personal export from their own profile. Personal exports contain only
+cards on boards the requesting person can access. Access is checked again when
+the export job runs.
+
+Exports are built in the background. Fizzy emails a private download link when
+the ZIP is ready. It expires **24 hours after completion**, even if the email is
+opened later. The ZIP is scheduled for removal at the same time. Treat the ZIP
+and its download link as confidential.
+
+## Importing
+
+Sign in and visit **`/account/imports/new`**, without an account URL prefix. Upload
+a whole-account Fizzy ZIP. A personal card export is not an account backup and
+cannot be imported. Importing creates a **new account**; it never merges into or
+overwrites an existing account.
+
+The importing identity becomes the new account's owner. People are matched by
+email address, or new identities are created. Identity credentials, sessions,
+access tokens, and staff privileges are not transferred. Inactive people remain
+inactive. Existing account owners become admins unless they are the importer.
+The importing owner receives access to all restored boards.
+
+Board publication keys and webhook secrets are regenerated. Webhooks are restored
+in a disabled state, so importing cannot send data to an unreviewed endpoint.
+Review and enable them in the new account if needed.
+
+Fizzy emails either the new account link or a failure reason. Database changes
+are transactional; a failed import rolls them back and deletes file objects
+written by that attempt. Source uploads are purged after processing. Import
+status and failure information are retained, not a partial account. Retrying a
+completed job does not create another account.
+
+## Archive format (version 1)
+
+* `manifest.json` identifies the version, export kind, source account, and record
+  counts. Imports reject unsupported formats, missing record sets, and references
+  outside the archive.
+* `card_exports/<number>.json` contains human-readable card data, rich-text HTML,
+  comments, and a list of attached files with their filenames and archive paths.
+* Whole-account exports also contain `<table>/<id>.json` files for the records
+  enumerated by `Account::DataTransfer::Manifest`.
+* `files/<blob-id>` contains original attachment bytes. Original filenames are in
+  the JSON metadata, not used as filesystem paths.
+* Rich-text record JSON uses portable `fizzy:<type>:<id>` references in place of
+  instance-specific signed global IDs. Import rewrites them to new signed IDs
+  and attachment URLs. All database IDs and storage keys are regenerated.
+
+ZIP entry CRCs, attachment sizes and checksums are verified. Duplicate and unsafe
+entry names are rejected. No archive path is extracted onto the filesystem.
+
+## Storage and workers
+
+The feature uses the configured Active Storage backend and the normal Active
+Job workers and mail delivery. Run database migrations before deploying it.
+
+ZIP64 archives are supported. File payloads are streamed rather than loaded into
+memory. S3 exports use multipart streaming; imports read ranges from storage and
+stream file entries directly to S3. Disk storage uses temporary files: provision
+enough temporary space for an export ZIP or the largest individual imported
+file. HTTP multipart uploads also require temporary upload space on the web
+server; configure request size limits and timeouts for your expected archives.
+JSON records are limited to 32 MiB each and ZIP directory reads to 256 MiB; these
+bounds do not limit the aggregate size of attached files.
